First, let's find an expression for $\dfrac{d}{dx}[2g(x)-4h(x)+6]$ in terms of $g'$ and $h'$:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}[2g(x)-4h(x)+6]
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}[2g(x)]-\dfrac{d}{dx}[4h(x)]+\dfrac{d}{dx}(6)&&\gray{\text{Sum and difference rules}}
\\\\
&=2g'(x)-4h'(x)+0&&\gray{\text{Constant and constant multiple rules}}
\end{align}$

So we found that $\dfrac{d}{dx}[2g(x)-4h(x)+6]=2g'(x)-4h'(x)$.
